{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *## Early Life and Regency* Raymond III was born around 1140 and became count at a young age after his father's assassination in 1152. As he was a minor his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he was of age to rule. \n\nAfter Hodierna's regency ended Raymond III took control of the county and was involved in various conflicts with neighboring states. In 1160 he allied himself with the Byzantine Empire and married Eschiva of Bures the widow of Walter of St Omer. In 1164 Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din Zangi while fighting in the Battle of Harim. He was released in 1173 after being ransomed.\n\n*## Leadership and Military Campaigns* During his leadership Raymond III was involved in several military campaigns against the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. He was a key figure in the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader forces were defeated by Saladin. Raymond III had advised against engaging the enemy in battle but his counsel was ignored.\n\nRaymond III's actions and decisions were often influenced by his desire to protect his county and maintain its independence. He was known for his military prowess and his ability to navigate the complex web of alliances and rivalries between the Crusader states. *## Legacy* After the Battle of Hattin Raymond III fled to Tripoli where he died in 1187. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy is complex and multifaceted with some historians viewing him as a skilled leader while others see him as indecisive. He played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states and his actions had far-reaching consequences for the region. Raymond III